'' AN ENUM IS INTEGER-SIZED, AND ITS PRINT ENTRY POINT HAS TO BE TOO.
''
'' Measured against fbc:
''
''     enum E : A = 1 : B = 4000000000 : end enum
''     print sizeof( E )   ->  8            on win64
''     print E.B           ->  4000000000
''
'' PrintRtlFor sent every enum through fb_PrintInt, the 32-bit entry point, while the
'' operand itself was i64. That is not a wrong number: clang refused the module with
'' "'%t6' defined with type 'i64' but expected 'i32'", so nothing printed at all. It is
'' what 'FsError.Code( )' printed through in the corpus, and the line that declined
'' does not mention an enum.
''
'' THE VALUE had a second, independent truncation behind it: an enum member's value
'' lives in Symbol.payload, which was a LONG, and the constant folder read it back at
'' 32 bits. Both widened, 'print E.B' gives 4000000000 rather than -294967296.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --emit-ir tests/codegen/src/print-enum.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0

'' THE ENTRY POINT, declared at its own width.
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare void @"fb_PrintLongint"(i32, i64, i32)

'' AND NOT the 32-bit one, which is what the enum used to take.
'' EXPECT-NOT: @"fb_PrintInt"(i32 0, i32
'' GOLDEN: expected/print-enum.out
